Z||000001||Fierokid87||12-22-2001||05:21 PM||||Best person to ask that would be skitime.  He has had the back window out in his GT for a custom removable window.<P>Scott<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="red"> Fierokid's  Web site:</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="white">http://www.angelfire.com/sports/sfiero</FONT><P><FONT COLOR="blue">AIM: Fierokid87<BR>ICQ: Fierokid</FONT><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierokid.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||12-23-2001||09:29 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierokid87:<BR><B>Best person to ask that would be skitime.  He has had the back window out in his GT for a custom removable window.<P>Scott<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, on the bathroom scale it is 17 lbs.  My advise is not use lexan but a product called MarGaurd.  I is actully lexan with a spayed on coating of real glass.  It is very clear and scratch resistant but can be cut like lexan. Oh and very expensive too.   <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 12-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||ITB 2M4||12-23-2001||01:47 PM||||The real question is, do you really want to remove the rear window.  Having done it before to put lexan in (my rollcage goes back to the rear strut towers for bracing), I can tell you right now, it's not a pleasant job!  The best way is during the winter, that way the 'sticky black junk' they use to keep the window in can be cut easier.  A PVC pipe cutter (wire) and a sharp razor is what I used.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||1
